WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.218
"This is NeuPL, or Neural Population Learning,

00:00:05.298 --> 00:00:10.016
published at ICLR 2022 by researchers at University
College London and DeepMind.

00:00:10.096 --> 00:00:14.813
Learning to play strategy games like StarCraft or
poker requires discovering a whole population of diverse policies,

00:00:14.893 --> 00:00:19.611
not just one.

00:00:19.691 --> 00:00:24.409
The usual recipe grows this population by
iteratively training a fresh policy to beat the previous ones,

00:00:24.489 --> 00:00:29.207
but in real games that approach wastes compute
relearning basic skills at every step and

00:00:29.287 --> 00:00:34.004
produces under-trained policies when budgets run out.

00:00:34.084 --> 00:00:38.802
NeuPL solves both problems by representing the
entire population inside a single conditional neural network,

00:00:38.882 --> 00:00:43.600
so skills transfer freely across policies while
still enjoying convergence guarantees."

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:47.943
"Classical population-learning methods such as
Policy Space Response Oracles grow a set

00:00:48.023 --> 00:00:51.985
of strategies by repeatedly training a new policy
to best-respond to a mixture over the

00:00:52.065 --> 00:00:56.028
existing ones.

00:00:56.108 --> 00:01:00.071
In toy normal-form games this works cleanly because
best-responses can be solved exactly.

00:01:00.151 --> 00:01:04.114
But real-world games are temporal and partially observed,

00:01:04.194 --> 00:01:08.156
so best-responses can only be approximated with
expensive deep reinforcement learning.

00:01:08.236 --> 00:01:12.199
This creates two failures: under a finite budget
you cannot tell a truly converged best-response

00:01:12.279 --> 00:01:16.242
from one stuck at a local plateau,

00:01:16.322 --> 00:01:20.285
so iterations get truncated prematurely and pollute
the population with weak policies;

00:01:20.365 --> 00:01:24.327
and every new policy relearns basic skills from scratch,

00:01:24.407 --> 00:01:28.370
which becomes intractable as opponents grow stronger."

00:01:28.750 --> 00:01:33.528
"The need for a population of strategies is rooted
in game theory: in a purely cyclic

00:01:33.608 --> 00:01:37.128
game like rock-paper-scissors a single strategy is
meaningless,

00:01:37.208 --> 00:01:40.615
since improving against one opponent means losing
to another.

00:01:40.695 --> 00:01:47.073
Prior frameworks handle this by training policies
one at a time and discarding the shared knowledge between them.

00:01:47.153 --> 00:01:51.645
NeuPL's insight is that these policies overlap
enormously they share perception,

00:01:51.725 --> 00:01:55.531
memory, and motor skills so training them
independently is wasteful.

00:01:55.611 --> 00:02:01.589
If a single model could hold the whole population
and condition its behaviour on which opponents it faces,

00:02:01.669 --> 00:02:06.447
early skills learned against weak opponents would
directly bootstrap the discovery of

00:02:06.527 --> 00:02:08.390
exploiters to much stronger ones."

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:12.142
"NeuPL makes three contributions.

00:02:12.222 --> 00:02:15.593
First,

00:02:15.673 --> 00:02:19.045
it is a single conditional model that represents a
whole population of policies,

00:02:19.125 --> 00:02:22.497
conditioned on a meta-game mixture strategy that
specifies which opponents each policy should beat.

00:02:22.577 --> 00:02:25.948
Second,

00:02:26.028 --> 00:02:29.400
it is a unifying framework: by choosing the
interaction graph you recover self-play,

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.852
fictitious play, or PSRO-Nash as special cases,

00:02:32.932 --> 00:02:36.303
and you can even express cyclic graphs that PSRO cannot.

00:02:36.383 --> 00:02:39.755
Third, it comes with convergence guarantees for grounded,

00:02:39.835 --> 00:02:43.207
lower-triangular interaction graphs and a suitable
meta-graph solver,

00:02:43.287 --> 00:02:46.658
NeuPL provably converges to an N-step best-response
and, with large enough N,

00:02:46.738 --> 00:02:50.110
to a normal-form Nash equilibrium."

00:02:50.490 --> 00:02:54.059
"Concretely, NeuPL trains one opponent-conditioned
network.

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:58.760
Each policy index i is paired with a meta-strategy
vector sigma-i that says,

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.398
as a probability distribution, which other policies
it should play against.

00:03:03.478 --> 00:03:07.481
The network maximizes discounted return under a
double expectation

00:03:07.561 --> 00:03:09.460
first over the sampled opponent,

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.738
then over the game dynamics induced by both policies.

00:03:12.818 --> 00:03:15.397
To optimize this by reinforcement learning,

00:03:15.477 --> 00:03:19.541
NeuPL jointly trains an opponent-conditioned
action-value function,

00:03:19.621 --> 00:03:25.170
and additionally learns a payoff estimator that
predicts the expected outcome of any policy

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:27.705
pairing directly from the value function,

00:03:27.785 --> 00:03:32.839
so the full payoff matrix can be evaluated cheaply
without replaying every matchup.

00:03:32.919 --> 00:03:36.921
The interaction graph that assigns opponents can be
a fixed matrix

00:03:37.001 --> 00:03:39.395
reproducing fictitious play or adaptive,

00:03:39.475 --> 00:03:44.467
recomputed from the learned payoffs by a meta-graph
solver, reproducing PSRO-Nash.

00:03:44.547 --> 00:03:49.662
Because the graph, not a handcrafted schedule,
controls which objectives are active,

00:03:49.742 --> 00:03:54.610
the number of genuinely distinct policies grows on
its own as the game demands."

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:58.706
"NeuPL is validated across three domains chosen to
span the difficulty spectrum.

00:03:58.786 --> 00:04:02.503
Rock-paper-scissors is the classic purely cyclic
normal-form game,

00:04:02.583 --> 00:04:06.299
where the learned population can be visualized
directly on the strategy simplex.

00:04:06.379 --> 00:04:10.095
Running-with-scissors lifts this into a spatiotemporal,

00:04:10.175 --> 00:04:13.892
partially observed setting: players move on a grid,
collect rock, paper,

00:04:13.972 --> 00:04:17.688
and scissors resources,

00:04:17.768 --> 00:04:21.485
and must infer a hidden opponent inventory from a
narrow first-person view.

00:04:21.565 --> 00:04:25.281
Finally,

00:04:25.361 --> 00:04:29.077
MuJoCo Football is a large-scale Game-of-Skills
where two-versus-two teams must simultaneously

00:04:29.157 --> 00:04:32.874
master continuous motor control and coordinated team play,

00:04:32.954 --> 00:04:36.670
a regime where handcrafted PSRO truncation is
especially fragile."

00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:41.254
"The headline result is that NeuPL is both more
efficient and more robust than comparable PSRO baselines.

00:04:41.334 --> 00:04:45.538
With a maximum population of eight policies,

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:49.822
a NeuPL population successfully exploits PSRO
populations of the same eight policies

00:04:49.902 --> 00:04:54.106
even when each PSRO iteration was given twice as
many gradient steps.

00:04:54.186 --> 00:04:58.390
Crucially,

00:04:58.470 --> 00:05:02.674
the gain in relative population performance
coincides with growth in the effective population size,

00:05:02.754 --> 00:05:06.958
from five up to eight distinct policies,

00:05:07.038 --> 00:05:11.242
showing that the improvement comes from genuinely
discovering new strategies rather than overfitting.

00:05:11.322 --> 00:05:15.526
Both continued-training and from-scratch PSRO
variants prove equally exploitable,

00:05:15.606 --> 00:05:19.810
suggesting they fail to build reusable representations."

00:05:20.190 --> 00:05:22.956
"A key ablation isolates the role of transfer.

00:05:23.036 --> 00:05:28.277
NeuPL agents are re-initialized either from scratch
or by transferring the encoder and

00:05:28.357 --> 00:05:32.052
memory components of a network trained to epoch one
thousand,

00:05:32.132 --> 00:05:36.259
then tasked with beating fixed Nash mixtures of
increasing strength.

00:05:36.339 --> 00:05:39.291
Against an easily exploitable two-policy mixture,

00:05:39.371 --> 00:05:43.807
even the from-scratch agent eventually finds a
counter, just more slowly.

00:05:43.887 --> 00:05:47.458
But against competent mixtures over four or seven
policies,

00:05:47.538 --> 00:05:51.975
the randomly initialized agent fails outright
despite prolonged training,

00:05:52.055 --> 00:05:55.563
while the transferred agent readily discovers the exploit.

00:05:55.643 --> 00:05:59.647
This is NeuPL's most striking property: as the
population expands,

00:05:59.727 --> 00:06:03.050
discovering new strategies becomes easier, not harder."

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:09.084
"To put numbers on it: a NeuPL population capped at
eight policies beats PSRO populations

00:06:09.164 --> 00:06:13.529
of eight policies even when PSRO trained twice as
long per iteration.

00:06:13.609 --> 00:06:16.234
As relative population performance climbs,

00:06:16.314 --> 00:06:20.551
the effective number of distinct policies grows
from five to eight,

00:06:20.631 --> 00:06:25.511
and across different maximum caps the effective
size saturates around twelve.

00:06:25.591 --> 00:06:30.279
The transfer study spans Nash mixtures over two,
four, and seven policies,

00:06:30.359 --> 00:06:31.889
each repeated five times.

00:06:31.969 --> 00:06:39.040
Together these establish NeuPL as more
sample-efficient and more robust than the standard iterative baselines."

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:44.500
"The one-line takeaway: represent the whole
population in a single conditional model,

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:47.961
let the interaction graph decide who trains against whom,

00:06:48.041 --> 00:06:50.693
and skills transfer for free across policies.

00:06:50.773 --> 00:06:55.307
This makes population learning cheaper, gives it
convergence guarantees, and

00:06:55.387 --> 00:06:59.374
most surprisingly makes novel strategies more
accessible, not less,

00:06:59.454 --> 00:07:01.377
as the neural population expands,

00:07:01.457 --> 00:07:05.020
from rock-paper-scissors all the way up to MuJoCo
Football."
